SYNTHESIS
======================================================================================
1. The LRC unit boundary skeleton is the same object as the odd top row:
   initial segments leave exactly phi(n) unit endpoints.
2. The first row doubling b->2b is a seam: it creates quotient/nonunit
   room but no new unit witnesses.  Later doublings double the unit
   skeleton.  This mirrors the tournament pair anomaly.
3. For even n, lpd(n)=n/2.  Thus the most visible multiplicative LRC
   descent is literally the row parent in the 2-adic grid.
4. The proof strategy suggested by the grid is two-dimensional:
   prove top-row +2 movement with unit/product-sum controls, and prove
   row movement with endpoint-debt transfer from n/2 to n.
5. In this language, n=14 is the first prime-core seam 7->14; n=16 is
   the pure dyadic row lab; n=18 is the first square-odd-core seam 9->18.
